From: FineWine
Subject: [PATCH] ntdll: Keep a file in the prefix open, so its processes can be found.

Wine replaces a process's arguments with its Windows command line, which also overwrites the
environment that "ps" would show, so nothing tells the outside world which prefix a Wine
process belongs to. Wine's server knows, and normally ends a prefix's processes itself, but
that is no help when the server is the one that is stuck or gone.

Every process now opens <prefix>/.finewine-alive and keeps it open for as long as it lives.
"lsof -t" on that file lists the prefix's processes, which is how FineWine's Force Shut Down
finds a program that has frozen. The descriptor is closed on exec, so only Wine processes
hold it.

diff --git a/dlls/ntdll/unix/loader.c b/dlls/ntdll/unix/loader.c
index 9e44f8a..4821ca1 100644
--- a/dlls/ntdll/unix/loader.c
+++ b/dlls/ntdll/unix/loader.c
@@ -422,6 +422,18 @@ static void init_paths(void)
     set_system_dll_path();
     set_home_dir();
     set_config_dir();
+
+    /* FineWine: every process of a prefix keeps this file open for as long as it lives. Wine
+     * replaces a process's arguments with its Windows command line, so nothing else tells
+     * the outside world which prefix a process belongs to; with this, "lsof" does, and a
+     * program that has frozen can be found and ended (FineWine's Force Shut Down). */
+    if (config_dir)
+    {
+        char *alive = build_path( config_dir, ".finewine-alive" );
+        int fd = open( alive, O_RDONLY | O_CREAT, 0644 );
+        if (fd != -1) fcntl( fd, F_SETFD, FD_CLOEXEC );
+        free( alive );
+    }
 }
 
 
